## Use the local adapter

Select the qualified package and verify its provenance first. Supply your authenticated executable digest and absolute paths; placeholders below must be replaced. This canonical fragment is for an already declared api workload.

```ts
import { LocalCLIClient } from "@maldous/cq-sdk";

const cli = await LocalCLIClient.create({
  executablePath: "/opt/cq/cq",
  expectedSHA256: "<64 lower-case hex characters>",
  configPath: "/srv/cq/cq.json",
  stateDirectory: "/srv/cq/state",
  environment: { LANG: "C" }
});

await cli.version(); // requires a compatible v1 executable
await cli.configSet("/workloads/api/environment", { APP_MODE: "test" });
await cli.doctor();
await cli.plan();
```

The child receives only the explicit environment; no shell is used. configSet sends JSON on stdin. run/flowRun inherit child output and report normal exit codes, including application failure. Call updateCheck explicitly if needed; the SDK does not schedule or install updates.

## Handle results and choose MCP

Handle safe error categories and propagate cancellation through your application. Do not log raw values or repeat an uncertain action merely because a client timed out. For MCP, create and authenticate the official session yourself, then use the language adapter shown in the canonical README. Session authentication does not grant provider access or plaintext disclosure. Source examples are not proof of package publication or live-provider qualification.
